"""Class 9 reading: extension paragraphs for P1 (brings each discursive passage to the 400-450 word range) + 2 extra sub-questions (P1 = 12 marks)."""
from eng9_lib import S
def m(q, o): return S(q, "a", 1, o)
EXT = {}; XS = {}
EXT[1] = """<p>Sleep also shapes the way we feel about other people. A tired student is quick to take offence, slow to forgive and unwilling to listen. Teachers often notice that a class which has slept well works in an atmosphere of patience, while a sleepless class turns small disagreements into quarrels. Athletes know this too; coaches of young players insist on rest before a match as firmly as they insist on practice.</p>
<p>Parents can help by setting an example. A home in which the television glows until midnight cannot expect a child to sleep at nine. Families that agree on a quiet hour, keep phones outside the bedroom and eat dinner together find that the whole house sleeps better. Rest, in the end, is not the opposite of work; it is the part of work that is done silently in the dark.</p>"""
XS[1] = [m("According to the added paragraphs, a class that has slept well works in an atmosphere of:", ["patience", "noise", "fear", "competition"]),
         S("How can parents help children sleep better?", "By setting an example, for instance keeping the television off late at night and phones outside the bedroom.", 1)]
EXT[2] = """<p>Saving water begins with small, ordinary acts. A tap left running while brushing teeth wastes several litres in a minute. A leaking pipe can lose hundreds of litres in a single day without anyone noticing. Washing a vehicle with a bucket instead of a hose, and watering plants with the water used to rinse vegetables, are simple ways of making every drop do two jobs.</p>
<p>Communities have done even more. In parts of Rajasthan, villagers revived old johads, small earthen dams that catch the monsoon rain, and found that wells which had been dry for years slowly filled again. Rooftop rainwater harvesting in schools and homes works on the same principle: water is not made, only moved, and the wise person moves it to where it will be needed. The lesson is that no city is too large and no village too small to begin.</p>"""
XS[2] = [m("A johad is a:", ["small earthen dam that catches rain", "type of well", "river boat", "water pump"]),
         S("Give one simple way mentioned of making every drop of water do two jobs.", "Watering plants with the water used to rinse vegetables.", 1)]
EXT[3] = """<p>The most useful thing about failure is that it is specific. A poor mark in mathematics does not say that a student is unintelligent; it points to a particular chapter that was not understood. When a cricketer is bowled out, the coach replays the shot and finds the exact movement of the feet that went wrong. Failure, seen this way, is not a verdict but a map, showing precisely where the road must be repaired.</p>
<p>It also teaches humility and patience, qualities no textbook can hand over. Those who have never failed often give up at the first difficulty, because they have no memory of climbing back. Those who have failed and recovered carry a quiet confidence: they know that a bad day is only a day. Schools can help by praising effort and by letting students correct their mistakes instead of hiding them.</p>"""
XS[3] = [m("The writer compares failure to a:", ["map showing where the road must be repaired", "closed door", "heavy bag", "bright lamp"]),
         S("What do people who have failed and recovered gain?", "A quiet confidence; they know a bad day is only a day.", 1)]
EXT[4] = """<p>The speed of news has brought great benefits. A cyclone warning reaches fishing villages within minutes, giving families time to move to safe places. Doctors in distant towns consult specialists through video calls, and a farmer can check the price of his crop in the city market before deciding where to sell. Information that once belonged to the rich and the educated now reaches almost everyone with a phone.</p>
<p>But speed has a price. A rumour travels as fast as a fact, and often faster, because it is simpler and more exciting. Before the sun sets, a false message can frighten a whole town. Wise readers therefore ask three questions before they believe or forward any report: who wrote it, what proof is given, and has any trustworthy source said the same? A moment of doubt is a small price to pay for the truth.</p>"""
XS[4] = [m("Which is NOT a benefit of fast news mentioned?", ["Rumours spread faster", "Cyclone warnings reach villages quickly", "Farmers can check crop prices", "Doctors can consult specialists"]),
         S("What three questions should a wise reader ask about a report?", "Who wrote it, what proof is given, and whether a trustworthy source has said the same.", 1)]
EXT[5] = """<p>The bicycle is also among the kindest machines for the environment. It needs no petrol, makes no smoke and takes only a fraction of the road space that a car occupies. Ten bicycles can be parked in the place where one car stands. A city that encourages cycling therefore breathes cleaner air, hears less noise and spends less on widening its roads.</p>
<p>Several towns have already shown the way. They have painted separate lanes for cyclists, built secure stands near bus stops and metro stations, and allowed riders to borrow a bicycle for a small fee and leave it at another stand. Where such lanes exist, children cycle to school without fear and older people take to the pedals for exercise. A bicycle, it seems, is not a machine of the past but a vehicle waiting for the right roads.</p>"""
XS[5] = [m("Ten bicycles can be parked in the space needed for:", ["one car", "two cars", "one bus", "one truck"]),
         S("Name one thing towns have done to encourage cycling.", "They painted cycle lanes, built secure stands, or started bicycle-sharing.", 1)]
EXT[6] = """<p>Look closely and every part of that skyline tells a story of its own. The well recalls a time when the whole village gathered at dawn, and the talk at its edge was the village's newspaper. The banyan marks the spot where elders settled disputes and children listened to tales. The temple or mosque spire records the faith of generations, and the school, the newest of the four, records the hope that tomorrow will be wider than yesterday.</p>
<p>When a village is modernised carelessly, these landmarks are the first to be lost. A road is widened and the old tree is cut; a concrete tank replaces the well; a loud speaker drowns the evening bell. Planners who want progress without loss must first walk through the village and listen to its people, for the people alone know which stones are only stones and which hold memory.</p>"""
XS[6] = [m("According to the passage, the talk at the village well was like the village's:", ["newspaper", "court", "school", "market"]),
         S("What should planners do before changing a village?", "Walk through the village and listen to its people.", 1)]
EXT[7] = """<p>Honesty needs courage because the cost is paid at once while the reward comes late. The student who confesses that he copied an answer faces the teacher's displeasure today; the benefit of a clear conscience and the teacher's trust appears only later. For this reason small acts of honesty are a training of the will. One who returns excess change to a shopkeeper is slowly becoming a person who will not be bought in larger matters.</p>
<p>Honesty is also a kindness. A friend who tells us gently that our essay is weak does us more good than ten who praise it. Truthful feedback lets us improve; false praise leaves us to discover our faults in front of strangers. The best honesty, therefore, is neither rude nor boastful; it speaks the truth at the right time, in the right tone and for the right reason, which is to help.</p>"""
XS[7] = [m("The writer says small acts of honesty are:", ["a training of the will", "a waste of time", "easy to perform", "rarely rewarded"]),
         S("Why is truthful feedback better than false praise?", "It lets us improve, while false praise leaves us to discover our faults in front of strangers.", 1)]
EXT[8] = """<p>The banyan also teaches a lesson in sharing. Its dense branches shelter birds, squirrels, bats and insects, while its small red figs feed them through the year. Beneath it, a tea seller sets up his stall, a cobbler spreads his tools and a child sits down to finish her homework in the shade. No one pays rent, and no one is turned away.</p>
<p>Environmentalists therefore call such trees keystones of the village ecosystem. When a banyan is cut for a wider road, the creatures that lived in it leave, the soil around it dries and the shade that cooled the lane disappears. Many villages now declare their old trees protected and tie a sacred thread around the trunk. The thread may be a tradition, but the idea behind it is a modern one: what shelters all deserves the protection of all.</p>"""
XS[8] = [m("Environmentalists call such trees:", ["keystones of the village ecosystem", "unwanted growth", "expensive shade", "dangerous"]),
         S("What happens when an old banyan is cut?", "The creatures living in it leave, the soil dries and the shade is lost.", 1)]
EXT[9] = """<p>Everyday heroism seldom makes headlines. Consider the bus conductor who waits patiently for an old woman to board, the nurse who stays beyond her shift because a patient is frightened, or the boy who shares his lunch with a classmate who has forgotten his own. None of them expects a medal. Each simply sees a need and decides that it is theirs to meet.</p>
<p>What unites such people is a quality of attention. They notice what others overlook, and, having noticed, they do not look away. Courage of this kind can be learned, for it grows with practice, as a muscle grows with use. A student who chooses to speak up when a classmate is mocked is already walking the road that leads to larger acts of bravery. A society is made strong not by a few great heroes but by thousands of small, steady ones.</p>"""
XS[9] = [m("What unites the heroes mentioned?", ["A quality of attention", "A love of medals", "Great wealth", "A military training"]),
         S("According to the passage, how can everyday courage be learned?", "It grows with practice, like a muscle grows with use.", 1)]
EXT[10] = """<p>When a language disappears, the loss is greater than a set of words. A tribal tongue may have thirty names for the stages of a single crop, or a song for each type of rain. Once its last speaker is gone, the knowledge held in those words goes with it, and no dictionary can bring it back. Linguists warn that nearly half of the world's languages may vanish within this century.</p>
<p>Yet there is reason for hope. Families in many places are teaching their mother tongues to children again, and schools are introducing folk songs and stories into the classroom. Technology, which often hurries languages towards silence, can also preserve them: recordings, fonts and mobile applications give small languages a place in the modern world. A language lives so long as someone is willing to speak it at home, and any child can be that someone.</p>"""
XS[10] = [m("Linguists warn that nearly half of the world's languages:", ["may vanish within this century", "are already extinct", "are growing rapidly", "are easy to preserve"]),
          S("How can technology help small languages?", "Through recordings, fonts and mobile applications that give them a place in the modern world.", 1)]

# third paragraph for each (to reach 400-450 words)
EXB = {
1: "<p>It is worth remembering that sleep cannot be saved up like pocket money. Sitting up all week and sleeping all Sunday does not repay the debt, because the brain needs its rest in regular instalments. Students preparing for examinations are often tempted to study through the night, yet those who revise in the evening and sleep well usually recall far more in the examination hall. A rested mind finds the answer quickly; a tired one searches in the dark for a word it knew yesterday. The best preparation, therefore, includes the pillow.</p>",
2: "<p>The question of fairness cannot be ignored either. A family in a posh colony may use a bucketful for a plant while a family in a slum queues for a single pot at the public tap. When supply is short, it is the poor who suffer first and longest. Governments must therefore mend leaking pipelines, price water sensibly and make sure that the last house in the lane is not forgotten. Citizens, in turn, must treat water as a shared trust rather than a private right to be spent without thought.</p>",
3: "<p>History offers many examples. Scientists whose experiments failed a hundred times have changed the world on the hundred and first. Writers whose manuscripts were rejected by publisher after publisher later found readers in millions. What they shared was not unusual talent but unusual stubbornness: each treated rejection as information, adjusted the method and tried again. Students need not dream of such fame to benefit from the example. The next time a test goes badly, the sensible question is not 'Why am I so poor?' but 'What exactly did I miss, and how shall I learn it?'</p>",
4: "<p>Schools can prepare young people for this world by teaching them to read the news the way a detective reads a scene. Compare two reports of the same event. Notice which words are chosen to excite and which to inform. Look for the date, the source and the number behind every claim. Such habits turn the reader from a passive listener into an active judge. In an age when anyone can publish, the most valuable skill is not speed in sharing but patience in checking, for a society that cannot tell fact from rumour cannot take sound decisions.</p>",
5: "<p>Cycling is good for the rider as well. Thirty minutes a day strengthens the heart, tones the legs and clears the mind, and unlike a gym it costs nothing after the first purchase. The rider also learns to notice his surroundings, such as the smell of rain on the road or the shape of a neighbourhood he only used to glimpse through a window. Perhaps that is why those who give up their cars for a few months often say that the city seemed to grow smaller, friendlier and far more interesting.</p>",
6: "<p>Children who grow up reading the skyline learn to treat their surroundings as a book rather than a backdrop. A class that draws a map of its village, interviews the oldest resident and records the history of the temple tank learns more about civics, geography and history than a month of lessons can teach. Such projects give young people a reason to stay connected with their roots, even when they leave for the city. A village that remembers its own story is never entirely poor, and a student who can tell it is never entirely a stranger anywhere.</p>",
7: "<p>Dishonesty, on the other hand, rarely stays alone. One lie needs a second to support it and a third to hide the second, until the liar spends more energy defending an untruth than he would have spent telling the truth. Sooner or later, the structure falls, and with it the trust that took years to build. Trust, once broken, is like a plate that has been mended: it may serve its purpose, but the crack is always visible. It is far wiser, and much lighter, to speak plainly from the start.</p>",
8: "<p>Children love such trees because they invite play: the hanging roots become swings, and the broad trunk becomes a fort. In the evening the same tree turns into a stage for the village, where stories are told and songs are sung and the day's quarrels are forgotten. Perhaps this is why sages in many ages chose to sit under a tree to think. Shade there is not merely protection from the sun but a gentle encouragement to slow down, look about and remember that human beings are only one among many dwellers on earth.</p>",
9: "<p>Schools could do more to recognise such quiet courage. Instead of honouring only the topper or the sportsman, an assembly might also thank the student who helped a new boy find his classroom, or the sweeper who quietly keeps the corridor clean. Such recognition tells children that goodness counts as much as achievement. It also tells them that heroism is within reach: it begins with noticing, continues with doing something small and becomes a habit. The hero, after all, is not someone unlike us but someone who acted when we merely watched.</p>",
10: "<p>Each of us can contribute. A grandmother's lullaby noted in a diary, a village proverb written on a school wall or a folk tale recorded on a phone is a small deposit in the bank of a community's memory. Young people who pride themselves on speaking several languages should include the language of their grandparents among them. Learning one more tongue is never a loss; it adds a new window to the mind. A person who can speak with the old and the young alike holds the living bridge on which a culture crosses into the future.</p>",
}
